1991 — Green Project
James Gosling and a small team at Sun Microsystems explore software for consumer devices.
1991 — Named "Oak"
The new language is named after an oak tree outside Gosling's office.
1992 — Star7 demo
A handheld prototype (?) shows Oak code running on custom hardware.
1995 — Renamed Java
"Oak" is already trademarked; Java is publicly announced in May 1995.
1996 — JDK 1.0
First official release (January 1996) — Java's mainstream..

Java has continuously evolved over the years through regular updates. Each major version introduced new features, improved performance and security, and made programming easier.
